WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.243
Secrets are high-risk application dependencies.

2
00:00:03.243 --> 00:00:04.623
A .

3
00:00:04.623 --> 00:00:25.183
NET application may depend on a value such as ExampleApiKey, ExampleConnectionString, ExampleCertificateName, ExampleSigningKeyName, ExampleWebhookSecret, or ExampleServiceIdentity to reach a database, call an API, sign data, decrypt protected content, publish messages, or authenticate to storage.

4
00:00:25.183 --> 00:00:32.427
If that value is exposed or misused, the application can lose control over important trust relationships.

5
00:00:32.427 --> 00:00:37.808
Secrets management belongs to design, development, deployment, and operations.

6
00:00:37.808 --> 00:00:41.741
Architects decide which systems need to trust each other.

7
00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:44.777
Developers decide how configuration is read.

8
00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:47.881
DevOps teams decide how values reach runtime.

9
00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:51.193
Operators monitor access and rotate credentials.

10
00:00:51.193 --> 00:00:59.196
A secret is not safe because one team handled one step correctly; it is safe when the whole lifecycle is controlled.

11
00:00:59.196 --> 00:01:10.373
Secrets influence database access, API integrations, storage, signing, encryption, messaging, webhooks, service-to-service communication, and operational tooling.

12
00:01:10.373 --> 00:01:21.619
They often sit quietly in configuration, but they determine whether a system can read data, modify records, publish messages, or prove identity to another service.

13
00:01:21.619 --> 00:01:27.415
It is useful to separate configuration values, sensitive configuration, and secrets.

14
00:01:27.415 --> 00:01:29.898
Some settings are ordinary behavior.

15
00:01:29.898 --> 00:01:35.556
Some are sensitive because they reveal internal design or change security posture.

16
00:01:35.556 --> 00:01:40.040
Secrets are values that can directly grant access or prove trust.

17
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:45.629
All three categories deserve clarity, but secrets require the strongest controls.

18
00:01:45.629 --> 00:01:53.839
Durable secrets management connects least privilege, rotation, monitoring, incident response, privacy, and reliability.

19
00:01:53.839 --> 00:01:56.875
Least privilege limits what a secret can do.

20
00:01:56.875 --> 00:02:00.324
Rotation and revocation reduce exposure over time.

21
00:02:00.324 --> 00:02:03.774
Monitoring and auditing show how secrets are used.

22
00:02:03.774 --> 00:02:08.880
Incident readiness lets the team respond without guessing during pressure.
